0,$ $y=1,$ $y=2,$ $z=0,$ $z=1.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=xy\\,i+2y\\,j+{x}^{2}zk,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}z) \\\\  & =y+2+{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+y+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}({x}^{2}+y+2)\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,{x}^{2}+y+2\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-162581/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a3fd63f-9991-5781-b4f8-9c4b0abc65ec"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-162581"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186134058 :question/answer-fields 17592186130911] {:db/id "q-162408/selection/ma-v1/c6e8e2eac91c866a5f975cf2a1d08baf43b449868fc0854a91e86e3417a2c44d/aac194b57ca9c96660533b3ab0fc43e597996e72b786d11bee5849f3d540c0f1" :answer/id #uuid "37e9ace8-60d1-5653-9f3e-743821adccfa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-2z}^{4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\\,2y+1\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162408/selection/ma-v1/c6e8e2eac91c866a5f975cf2a1d08baf43b449868fc0854a91e86e3417a2c44d/210ae1dd56fe0670fd3f4e82ae02520ba45b5aca5d8a203d23a34e68963988cb" :answer/id #uuid "ea5bfdd6-0806-5a67-8e3e-b18a2af64a34" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}^{y-2z}\\,2y+1\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162408/selection/ma-v1/c6e8e2eac91c866a5f975cf2a1d08baf43b449868fc0854a91e86e3417a2c44d/c90213a6203f9db93193e5bbe1e951a68fb81450a377fd6d80baffffe92f6aa1" :answer/id #uuid "8bb0cea1-145d-5c9e-a6d6-b83c4f4b97e3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}^{y-2z}\\,{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162408/selection/ma-v1/c6e8e2eac91c866a5f975cf2a1d08baf43b449868fc0854a91e86e3417a2c44d/1d39deebc2d3bb8a779bfc963cb963bccf58fcb1f28d8f3ed11e4904622af178" :answer/id #uuid "d2eb57e2-583e-5ae5-b1e6-4b195a14601f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-2z}^{4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\\,{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162408/selection/ma-v1/c6e8e2eac91c866a5f975cf2a1d08baf43b449868fc0854a91e86e3417a2c44d/29d285c54e32d9ab63fa0d4fcef981a529848938c580f3cc9f5732959577c25d" :answer/id #uuid "2c222030-976a-5a50-b59b-c7eaabdf5540" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-2}^{4}\\,z+3y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162408/selection/ma-v1/c6e8e2eac91c866a5f975cf2a1d08baf43b449868fc0854a91e86e3417a2c44d" :answer-field/id #uuid "5602e98c-f1af-58b7-98ca-82b8eabdd4f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-162408/selection/ma-v1/c6e8e2eac91c866a5f975cf2a1d08baf43b449868fc0854a91e86e3417a2c44d/1d39deebc2d3bb8a779bfc963cb963bccf58fcb1f28d8f3ed11e4904622af178" :answer-field/choices ["q-162408/selection/ma-v1/c6e8e2eac91c866a5f975cf2a1d08baf43b449868fc0854a91e86e3417a2c44d/aac194b57ca9c96660533b3ab0fc43e597996e72b786d11bee5849f3d540c0f1" "q-162408/selection/ma-v1/c6e8e2eac91c866a5f975cf2a1d08baf43b449868fc0854a91e86e3417a2c44d/210ae1dd56fe0670fd3f4e82ae02520ba45b5aca5d8a203d23a34e68963988cb" "q-162408/selection/ma-v1/c6e8e2eac91c866a5f975cf2a1d08baf43b449868fc0854a91e86e3417a2c44d/c90213a6203f9db93193e5bbe1e951a68fb81450a377fd6d80baffffe92f6aa1" "q-162408/selection/ma-v1/c6e8e2eac91c866a5f975cf2a1d08baf43b449868fc0854a91e86e3417a2c44d/1d39deebc2d3bb8a779bfc963cb963bccf58fcb1f28d8f3ed11e4904622af178" "q-162408/selection/ma-v1/c6e8e2eac91c866a5f975cf2a1d08baf43b449868fc0854a91e86e3417a2c44d/29d285c54e32d9ab63fa0d4fcef981a529848938c580f3cc9f5732959577c25d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-162408"] :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $y=-1,$ $y=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=1,$ and the surfaces $x=y-2z$ and $x=4-{z}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=x{y}^{2}\\,i+xz\\,j+y\\,k,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(y) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}+0+0 \\\\  & ={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type II solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,y-2z\\le x\\le 4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-2z}^{4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\\,{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-162408/selection/ma-v1/c6e8e2eac91c866a5f975cf2a1d08baf43b449868fc0854a91e86e3417a2c44d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-162588/selection/bfa9e10a8dd5ecd0154164d12f84752d1dbe026e4d48618fdcac72de2b00d96f" :answer/id #uuid "33270575-6686-533e-a787-ee503a855d8e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2y}^{2{x}^{2}+1}\\,{x}^{2}-1\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162588/selection/b899cd64a75cc94507009742f41d308e1822914b55ac9b360abfbfd182ba404d" :answer/id #uuid "95f2bbf7-051c-57da-9c30-16a10e22bb41" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,2xz\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162588/selection/cbb12d989128c8a03c73279e2ae13e9bde8bc93aac2c0e3e47830701e1dfd886" :answer/id #uuid "1455dc99-7527-5fac-8ea9-65123797883f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2y}^{2{x}^{2}+1}\\,y+z-2\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162588/selection/481ec85f9ac4fcd3ad058425ffae66ce54ccb2bf0813051cd23815234b8c75c4" :answer/id #uuid "12983a0d-93e7-558b-ba73-b4d108c6cba0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,{x}^{2}-1\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162588/selection/0ca1483143d1884ec82b4a2d8e23407cf2f62bd0814cdc228b67967c4912874f" :answer/id #uuid "a53e62d2-c3c1-5bf3-b7ed-f50ed4de2b07" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,y+z-2\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162588/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f364bf57-698c-59e1-9fee-d884c9fd3252" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-162588/selection/481ec85f9ac4fcd3ad058425ffae66ce54ccb2bf0813051cd23815234b8c75c4" :answer-field/choices ["q-162588/selection/bfa9e10a8dd5ecd0154164d12f84752d1dbe026e4d48618fdcac72de2b00d96f" "q-162588/selection/b899cd64a75cc94507009742f41d308e1822914b55ac9b360abfbfd182ba404d" "q-162588/selection/cbb12d989128c8a03c73279e2ae13e9bde8bc93aac2c0e3e47830701e1dfd886" "q-162588/selection/481ec85f9ac4fcd3ad058425ffae66ce54ccb2bf0813051cd23815234b8c75c4" "q-162588/selection/0ca1483143d1884ec82b4a2d8e23407cf2f62bd0814cdc228b67967c4912874f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-162588" :question/id #uuid "07b2df57-316b-561c-bce7-7ecfebe212fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-162588" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=2,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ and the surfaces $z=y$ and $z={x}^{2}+y.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=(x+y)\\,i-2y\\,j+{x}^{2}z\\,k,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}z) \\\\  & =1+(-2)+{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}({x}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type I solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,y\\le z\\le {x}^{2}+y}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}-1\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,{x}^{2}-1\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-162588/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a3fd63f-9991-5781-b4f8-9c4b0abc65ec"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-162588"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-162569/selection/1a2c3881891ab7eca8aa4af8d7002d84b8252f633df18da1be453e64ebd8a8b4" :answer/id #uuid "161693cd-c882-54e3-92be-61f5c5fc7afb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162569/selection/91e4518bf275e5cd0e98958f5351f8fe7592fcaaea11295f89e7442905e7ffe4" :answer/id #uuid "42b46116-de75-5acd-9690-4e12c330929d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-12\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162569/selection/0292053b265ef4b990d6838606671ab0887e46c3e5929ffc1ff8dc100207103f" :answer/id #uuid "e853cda6-c206-5e40-bcb4-802df8153563" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-16\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162569/selection/181f69e2ddda97cdc36f295b113d0a8a7a6e23842533f39b3be526bc60f94b57" :answer/id #uuid "dd0ee171-3960-5d62-b7dc-3048dcb45d12" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162569/selection/cd8957b3e03d9fbea87893281373eded912abde9aae6462917a9ce8659b8cc99" :answer/id #uuid "0d7adaa7-91c0-5947-849b-6e32a6a5d351" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162569/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ec78130e-b332-5bf6-9e83-6fc29a18b6ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-162569/selection/91e4518bf275e5cd0e98958f5351f8fe7592fcaaea11295f89e7442905e7ffe4" :answer-field/choices ["q-162569/selection/1a2c3881891ab7eca8aa4af8d7002d84b8252f633df18da1be453e64ebd8a8b4" "q-162569/selection/91e4518bf275e5cd0e98958f5351f8fe7592fcaaea11295f89e7442905e7ffe4" "q-162569/selection/0292053b265ef4b990d6838606671ab0887e46c3e5929ffc1ff8dc100207103f" "q-162569/selection/181f69e2ddda97cdc36f295b113d0a8a7a6e23842533f39b3be526bc60f94b57" "q-162569/selection/cd8957b3e03d9fbea87893281373eded912abde9aae6462917a9ce8659b8cc99"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-162569" :question/id #uuid "cccfe8f0-fae6-5eb5-97b7-607f248d5344" :question/math-academy-id "q-162569" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/assets/581ac462bfcfead22a6c5ad4eb247f05adb1a677e92cac51954b0746cd083ab7.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨x-y,\\,y+z,\\,2x-3z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $3$ whose base is a circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Given that $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y+z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2x-3z) \\\\  & =1+1+(-3) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid cylinder enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}-1\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =-{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =-\\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =-\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-12\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-162569/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c239481-1424-575a-b4f7-b6f5409dd240"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-162569"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-162572/selection/e326a281f601aec64d5dcce4921033c5f93cbf7f8b0a7a5809a9962dfadde666" :answer/id #uuid "91817b8b-b2af-58d6-99cd-23dead19d030" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162572/selection/f345fac9a81ccd096b9959c6e7a966a446e93e73b82d111a376f9064132509fd" :answer/id #uuid "c4c9d965-5bd1-506c-b3b4-1a1f96cd8f8f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "24" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162572/selection/650efaaa01beaa7de6c030497edff1dd29c570bf683f3726bbcacabc3dfa7e4c" :answer/id #uuid "68efd64f-4583-553c-ad4a-b8f9a8843333" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162572/selection/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" :answer/id #uuid "17c8f4c9-337e-549f-9444-4204dc6085de" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162572/selection/801bcdd9cb6a10cec998162e05f71050c8aaf0c6974f095bdcaef715a09f4aee" :answer/id #uuid "b861f96d-c51c-571c-82e0-8bac5a5eda7b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "21" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162572/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "dd8f3189-2922-5ad5-80f7-e76b43bad640" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-162572/selection/e326a281f601aec64d5dcce4921033c5f93cbf7f8b0a7a5809a9962dfadde666" :answer-field/choices ["q-162572/selection/e326a281f601aec64d5dcce4921033c5f93cbf7f8b0a7a5809a9962dfadde666" "q-162572/selection/f345fac9a81ccd096b9959c6e7a966a446e93e73b82d111a376f9064132509fd" "q-162572/selection/650efaaa01beaa7de6c030497edff1dd29c570bf683f3726bbcacabc3dfa7e4c" "q-162572/selection/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" "q-162572/selection/801bcdd9cb6a10cec998162e05f71050c8aaf0c6974f095bdcaef715a09f4aee"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-162572" :question/id #uuid "7c2bde26-4c23-54bd-9680-448652feb44d" :question/math-academy-id "q-162572" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/assets/aac27058402f89bef73700129a7812b3cf4f971c11975fc1711a46b8d10e61fe.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨3x+y{e}^{z},\\,xy+2,\\,1-xz⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Given that $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x+y{e}^{z})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy+2)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(1-xz) \\\\  & =3+x+(-x) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the rectangular solid enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}3\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =3{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1\\cdot 2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-162572/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c239481-1424-575a-b4f7-b6f5409dd240"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-162572"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-162580/selection/749eff2985363dccc5bb12451aaaed76442e7a824c86bae23bd6cb3d8de20d0d" :answer/id #uuid "0d52b91e-ccf6-58f2-b6b6-81c579b27d80" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{32\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162580/selection/8a062f85dfae2453091ecd95389dc9b0751d8bb7c18126361428cf17100dc6b2" :answer/id #uuid "576b65e8-29bb-5e90-9496-d6481c6e4fac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{16\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162580/selection/fcc327425bffa26181c03d4b5128695b2377c88b52a7f5521a7198aa3c9e78a0" :answer/id #uuid "96b6bc14-4c42-50a6-bc5d-37113c5dc0f8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162580/selection/e5c63bc832c49e18fb2678001d953240c26767b2f942793f012ef9f6442d3680" :answer/id #uuid "4d5b5e91-ad46-570d-884d-51d779981a44" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{8\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162580/selection/0fb978d2b1ce374ae0f1700c585078833e85991af693baf7445a5e2dc6cdf6cc" :answer/id #uuid "e638f438-e1e3-56c9-bd6b-174ba4f5d383" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162580/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "bf337090-c8b2-5aea-bf56-8bd215441650" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-162580/selection/8a062f85dfae2453091ecd95389dc9b0751d8bb7c18126361428cf17100dc6b2" :answer-field/choices ["q-162580/selection/749eff2985363dccc5bb12451aaaed76442e7a824c86bae23bd6cb3d8de20d0d" "q-162580/selection/8a062f85dfae2453091ecd95389dc9b0751d8bb7c18126361428cf17100dc6b2" "q-162580/selection/fcc327425bffa26181c03d4b5128695b2377c88b52a7f5521a7198aa3c9e78a0" "q-162580/selection/e5c63bc832c49e18fb2678001d953240c26767b2f942793f012ef9f6442d3680" "q-162580/selection/0fb978d2b1ce374ae0f1700c585078833e85991af693baf7445a5e2dc6cdf6cc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-162580" :question/id #uuid "54b0961b-2c4a-5e03-819c-f8b7975f6411" :question/math-academy-id "q-162580" :question/problem "Consider the positively (outward) oriented surface $S$ given by $(x+2)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ and the vector field $F=({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\,i+4y\\,j+2xz\\,k.$ Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2xz) \\\\  & =-2x+4+2x \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid sphere of radius $1$ enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}4\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =4{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\cdot {1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-162580/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c239481-1424-575a-b4f7-b6f5409dd240"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-162580"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-162578/selection/4ee039236b0e5efb9f8d3a555e37cfbf65f492940c5b4115b68603b818d3edb0" :answer/id #uuid "679bb337-2eae-5325-b65f-15321136ae81" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{128\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162578/selection/8871a3cbbfe076fde6d33657cddd4968ea61752f79597602ffd81fdbd0736d76" :answer/id #uuid "10066137-c52f-5eb8-ad46-922eaeb7c60f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{256\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162578/selection/d71669493bfc65c051896c8c86c89d5ca62c6dbb821ff41f8fae0edbd649e46f" :answer/id #uuid "cacd617f-f18f-5e8a-8835-616879b95e55" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "96\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162578/selection/8b3b422a1a6a094d1e54b38a63948505a80038bf2714cbbe0a27a819e94a04b0" :answer/id #uuid "3d11e33c-5c19-5afd-be27-6c3f6a827ace" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "64\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162578/selection/f15f3a4723b1cfe6997f32aff1e57602fa2d789ab152ff4ca86843f839ca260e" :answer/id #uuid "eb5cc3ad-a5b9-5e7b-bf22-d76b02cab9e4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "78\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162578/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d50f0f5d-1a3a-5af5-bb0c-c2579e1e5951" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-162578/selection/d71669493bfc65c051896c8c86c89d5ca62c6dbb821ff41f8fae0edbd649e46f" :answer-field/choices ["q-162578/selection/4ee039236b0e5efb9f8d3a555e37cfbf65f492940c5b4115b68603b818d3edb0" "q-162578/selection/8871a3cbbfe076fde6d33657cddd4968ea61752f79597602ffd81fdbd0736d76" "q-162578/selection/d71669493bfc65c051896c8c86c89d5ca62c6dbb821ff41f8fae0edbd649e46f" "q-162578/selection/8b3b422a1a6a094d1e54b38a63948505a80038bf2714cbbe0a27a819e94a04b0" "q-162578/selection/f15f3a4723b1cfe6997f32aff1e57602fa2d789ab152ff4ca86843f839ca260e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-162578" :question/id #uuid "d8dbe31e-6bdd-543e-b097-44ed741785c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-162578" :question/problem "Consider the positively (outward) oriented surface $S$ given by ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+(z-4)^{2}=4$ and the vector field $F=z\\,i+6y\\,j+(x+3z)\\,k.$ Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x+3z) \\\\  & =0+6+3 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid sphere of radius $2$ enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}9\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =9{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{3} \\\\  & =96\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-162578/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c239481-1424-575a-b4f7-b6f5409dd240"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-162578"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-162410"] :question/problem "Consider the positively (outward) oriented surface $S$ given by ${x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}+(z+1)^{2}=25$ and the vector field $F=x\\,i+xz\\,j+3z\\,k.$ Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3z) \\\\  & =1+0+3 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid sphere of radius $5$ enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}4\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =4{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\cdot {5}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\,000\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-162606"] :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=2,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,j+(x+y)\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x+y) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+0+0 \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=2}\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot [\\operatorname{sin}⁡z]_{z=0}^{z=\\pi /2} \\\\  & =({2}^{2}-{0}^{2})\\cdot 1\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-162596/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "a179c58f-587f-5ba3-8b70-466cad576816" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162596/selection/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" :answer/id #uuid "f03ea61e-fb65-59bd-9087-891b44a6e5ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162596/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "8f337480-aa00-5ae0-8039-bda944fe767f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162596/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer/id #uuid "84a977cf-0aae-51e3-a516-b036d315e448" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162596/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "ec38f0f1-d0d7-5aed-9262-9fea22240afc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162596/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f256505b-c2ca-531e-96ca-e345eed18b67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-162596/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-162596/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-162596/selection/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" "q-162596/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-162596/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" "q-162596/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-162596" :question/id #uuid "b7f302e2-c66a-5f62-9bda-122fb92df5ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-162596" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=1,$ $x=e,$ $y=0,$ $y=2,$ $z=0,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,i+{z}^{2}\\,j+(x-{y}^{3})\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x-{y}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x}+0+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]_{x=1}^{x=e}\\cdot {[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\cdot [z]_{z=0}^{z=2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\cdot (\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2})\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-162596/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7866416-74af-5891-b8c9-f6c1a3a751ac"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-162596"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-162594/selection/286fb92e9ba180b012d241bb7e762701483c9c864420d9df6597147f2e2fb458" :answer/id #uuid "0104f547-7285-5a52-b44d-8271a0798e61" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "28" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162594/selection/cee37ae0a2921bd3973a5cb0592c822dbbfafcfefba8d7007910ead797b9112f" :answer/id #uuid "1a167791-55f1-5ac8-aebc-363172a73126" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "48" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162594/selection/713bb3b087b31554fba8b48520fba2f9d4d353e9b2d87f3d41c293daf48e1776" :answer/id #uuid "ebc479bd-f327-5b0f-9788-1b8eda54bdb2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "36" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162594/selection/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" :answer/id #uuid "a192554d-263f-515b-bd37-a122e23c8957" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162594/selection/650efaaa01beaa7de6c030497edff1dd29c570bf683f3726bbcacabc3dfa7e4c" :answer/id #uuid "0cb277df-23d2-51e0-a224-947f2c149adb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162594/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "77379f98-513e-50e3-a943-63caa4b9dafa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-162594/selection/cee37ae0a2921bd3973a5cb0592c822dbbfafcfefba8d7007910ead797b9112f" :answer-field/choices ["q-162594/selection/286fb92e9ba180b012d241bb7e762701483c9c864420d9df6597147f2e2fb458" "q-162594/selection/cee37ae0a2921bd3973a5cb0592c822dbbfafcfefba8d7007910ead797b9112f" "q-162594/selection/713bb3b087b31554fba8b48520fba2f9d4d353e9b2d87f3d41c293daf48e1776" "q-162594/selection/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" "q-162594/selection/650efaaa01beaa7de6c030497edff1dd29c570bf683f3726bbcacabc3dfa7e4c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-162594" :question/id #uuid "25c7ce92-3c53-5961-9526-9951885113bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-162594" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=2,$ $y=0,$ $y=4,$ $z=1,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F={x}^{2}y\\,i+2z\\,j+xyz\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xyz) \\\\  & =2xy+0+xy \\\\  & =3xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}3xy\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{2}3xy\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{4}y\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}3\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\cdot {[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=4}\\cdot [3z]_{z=1}^{z=2} \\\\  & =(2-0)\\cdot (8-0)\\cdot (6-3) \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-162594/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7866416-74af-5891-b8c9-f6c1a3a751ac"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-162594"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-162599/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer/id #uuid "61552f87-bae4-5e36-89d2-0529f45676bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162599/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "c4ce8af8-1cab-53b7-b4be-fbaab6ecc90b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162599/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "59fb4934-67cf-5be5-85c4-8b99d21b2f5a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162599/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "b4c2b43a-6198-54f7-8556-699ba097fda6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162599/selection/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" :answer/id #uuid "8908ab80-4a54-5c16-bebf-26d72f20ef7a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162599/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "42f89423-06ad-5708-9940-540ddda32b60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-162599/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer-field/choices ["q-162599/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" "q-162599/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-162599/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-162599/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-162599/selection/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-162599" :question/id #uuid "90fe91b8-bc99-59fe-8878-7778d578ba2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-162599" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=1,$ $y=1,$ $y=2,$ $z=1,$ $z=3.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F={x}^{2}\\,i+2y\\,j+(x+{y}^{2})\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2x+2+0 \\\\  & =2x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}(2x+2)\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(2x+2)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+2)\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{3}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}+2x]_{x=0}^{x=1}\\cdot (2-1)\\cdot (3-1) \\\\  & =({1}^{2}+2(1)-0)\\cdot 1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-162599/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7866416-74af-5891-b8c9-f6c1a3a751ac"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-162599"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-162600/selection/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" :answer/id #uuid "6336215b-4e92-5986-a1f2-f8308ce6b70c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162600/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer/id #uuid "91782d49-bdac-5103-9436-b5a3b8a1865c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162600/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "a749f4a7-9a8f-5425-8664-3be06b91d636" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162600/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "8083d565-6616-589b-a364-3e848a83ad8d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162600/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "8082c90c-262a-515c-bb00-959e0bfb6950" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-162600/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c15596a5-4ef4-51ee-bf37-5bd8df47d933" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-162600/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer-field/choices ["q-162600/selection/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" "q-162600/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" "q-162600/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-162600/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-162600/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-162600" :question/id #uuid "c34b8d27-a2d0-5c0a-b0c5-368721d0dede" :question/math-academy-id "q-162600" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=1,$ $y=1,$ $y=2,$ $z=-1,$ $z=1.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=2y\\,i+x{y}^{2}\\,j+2xyz\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2xyz) \\\\  & =0+2xy+2xy \\\\  & =4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}4xy\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}4xy\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}2y\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=1}\\cdot [{y}^{2}]_{y=1}^{y=2}\\cdot (1-(-1)) \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (4-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-162600/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7866416-74af-5891-b8c9-f6c1a3a751ac"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-162600"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-12447"] :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ and the surfaces $z=y$ and $z=1+x^2+y^2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F} = (x+z )\\, \\mathbf{i} + yz \\, \\mathbf{j} + x^2y \\, \\mathbf{k},$ express the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ as a triple integral, and then as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n= \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(yz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}y) \\\\  & =1+z+0 \\\\  & =z+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iiint\\limits_R (z+1) \\: \\textrm{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type I solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\:-1 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\: y \\leq z \\leq 1+x^2+y^2 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nExpressing our triple integral as a repeated integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z+1\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,z+1\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-12448"] :question/problem "Consider the positively oriented surface $S$ given by $x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 4$ and the vector field $\\mathbf{F} = 2x \\, \\mathbf{i} + y \\, \\mathbf{j} + 4 \\, \\mathbf{k}.$ Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n= \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4) \\\\  & =2+1+0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid sphere of radius $2$ enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}3\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =3{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{3} \\\\  & =32\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-12449"] :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=1,$ $y=0,$ $y=2,$ $z=1,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F} =e^z \\, \\mathbf{i} + x^2y^2 \\, \\mathbf{j} + 4x^2yz \\, \\mathbf{k},$ find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n= \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{z})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4{x}^{2}yz) \\\\  & =0+2{x}^{2}y+4{x}^{2}y \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}2y\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}]_{x=0}^{x=1}\\cdot [{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=2}\\cdot (2-1) \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (4-0)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
